    "q-158421": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158421",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Compute $P(-1<X<0)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12}({x}^{2}+2),\\, & -2\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & 1<x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X<0) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{1}{12}({x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+2x]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}([\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3}+2(0)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}+2(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(0+\\frac{1}{3}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{7}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
